{"id": "194281", "revid": "2217354", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=194281", "title": "Suri", "text": "Suri or Siuri (\u09b8\u09bf\u0989\u09a1\u09bc\u09c0 \"Siu\u1e5b\u012b\", pronounced \"SHEW-ree\") is the headquarters of the Birbhum district of West Bengal.\nEat.\nSuri is famous for a sweetmeat, morabba, and for pickles. Morabba is made of various vegetable/fruit items preserved in a sugar base: \"potoler motabba\", \"beler motabba\", \"chal kumror morabba\" (known as \"petha\" in Agra), \"sata mulir morabba\", \"amlokir morabba\" and so on. These are available in the sweetmeat shops."}
{"id": "194284", "revid": "2441334", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=194284", "title": "Prantik", "text": "Prantik (\u09aa\u09cd\u09b0\u09be\u09a8\u09cd\u09a4\u09bf\u0995 \"Pr\u0101ntik\") is a town north of Santiniketan, in the Birbhum district of West Bengal.\nGet in.\nBy car.\nPrantik is on Santiniketan Road, a long artery that starts from Bolpur."}
{"id": "194296", "revid": "2441334", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=194296", "title": "Arviat", "text": "Arviat (Inuktitut syllabics: \u140a\u1550\u1555\u140a\u1466) is Nunavut's third largest town with a population of 2,864 (2021).\nUnderstand.\nArviat is the southernmost community in mainland Nunavut, on the western edge of Hudson Bay. It is less than an hour from Churchill, Manitoba. People who live in Arviat are called Arviammiut. They are a proud people who live in harmony with the land and wildlife around them and maintain a strong connection to their traditions and Inuit culture. Two national historic sites, Arvia'juaq (an island shaped like a large bowhead whale) and Qikiqtaarjuk (a small island), are easily accessible from the community are testament to the rich heritage and cultural resources. \nThe town was known as Eskimo Point until 1989 when it was renamed Arviat, a name derived from an Inuktitut work, \"arviq\", meaning \"bowhead whale\" as the nearby coastal landscape resembles the shape of a bowhead whale.\nGet in.\nBy plane.\n () is served by Calm Air from Baker Lake, Chesterfield Inlet, Churchill, Rankin Inlet, Thompson, Whale Cove, and Winnipeg. Local taxis provide service between the town and the airport for $7.\nDrink.\nArviat is a dry town. Anyone caught in possession of alcohol can be arrested."}
{"id": "194310", "revid": "2441334", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=194310", "title": "Chase (British Columbia)", "text": "Chase is a village of approximately 2,400 people (2021) in the Shuswap region of British Columbia. It is known as the \"Gateway to the Shuswap\".\nUnderstand.\nChase is situated at the outlet Little Shuswap Lake at the western edge of the Shuswap Lake system, which is the primary attraction for the area. Chase Creek, which drops over three small waterfalls before flowing through the town, enters the South Thompson just below the lake's outlet.\nGet in.\nChase is located on the Highway 1 (Trans-Canada Highway), midway between Salmon Arm and Kamloops.\nGet around.\nA car is not necessary to explore Chase itself, and the town can be easily walked from end to end. \nBuy.\nChase has a couple smaller grocery stores as well as a few shops along its historic main street of Shuswap Avenue."}
