{"id": "194549", "revid": "2217354", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=194549", "title": "Bankura (district)", "text": "Bankura (\u09ac\u09be\u0981\u0995\u09c1\u09a1\u09bc\u09be \"B\u0101nku\u1e5b\u0101\") is a district of West Bengal.\nUnderstand.\nVisitor information.\nOfficial websites:\nGet in.\nBy train.\nBankura and Bishnupur are major railheads in the district."}
{"id": "194550", "revid": "2217354", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=194550", "title": "Purulia (district)", "text": "Purulia (\u09aa\u09c1\u09b0\u09c1\u09b2\u09bf\u09af\u09bc\u09be \"Puruliy\u0101\") is the westernmost district of West Bengal, blessed with their unique geography and culture.\nUnderstand.\nPurulia (\"POO-roo-lyah\") has the mixed culture of Bengal, Jharkhand and Odisha as it was a part of these regions for various times. From archaeological evidences to local festivals, every cultural event has got a tribal touch in it, which is the speciality of the district. Living mostly in rural areas and keeping intact many of their socio-cultural values, more or less in pristine forms, the countrymen have their folks to speak about many of their tenets.\nPurulia is an interesting blend of nature and history. The district has lush green mountain ranges with rocky outcrops. It is a place for adventure sports like rock climbing. During the winter rock climbing, camping and nature study courses are held in the outskirts of the district headquarters. Purulia also has a rich heritage, with several archeological sites of Hindu and Jain origin in the district.\nVisitor information.\nOfficial websites:"}
{"id": "194551", "revid": "2217354", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=194551", "title": "Paschim Bardhaman", "text": "Paschim Bardhaman (\u09aa\u09b6\u09cd\u099a\u09bf\u09ae \u09ac\u09b0\u09cd\u09a7\u09ae\u09be\u09a8 \"Poshchim Bordhom\u0101n\"), literally \"West Bardhaman\", is the western coal-rich industrial district of West Bengal. Fondly known as the \"Ruhr of India\", Paschim Bardhaman is home to two steel cities, a coal mining centre, and a locomotive workshop.\nUnderstand.\nPaschim Bardhaman is a major mining and industrial area in West Bengal. The area was once heavily forested and infested with plunderers and marauders for ages. The discovery of coal in the 18th century led to industrialisation, with cities like Asansol and Durgapur flourishing to become two of the most important industrial centres of Eastern India.\nVisitor information.\nOfficial websites:\nGet in.\nBy plane.\nKazi Nazrul Islam Airport () at Durgapur is the only airport in Paschim Bardhaman with commercial operations."}
{"id": "194552", "revid": "2253620", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=194552", "title": "Purba Bardhaman", "text": "Purba Bardhaman (\u09aa\u09c2\u09b0\u09cd\u09ac \u09ac\u09b0\u09cd\u09a7\u09ae\u09be\u09a8 \"P\u016brbo Bordhom\u0101n\"), literally \"East Bardhaman\", is a fertile district of central West Bengal. It is bordered by Birbhum and Murshidabad on the north, Nadia on the east, Hooghly on the south, Bankura on the southwest, and Paschim Bardhaman on the west.\nUnderstand.\nVisitor information.\nOfficial websites:\nSee.\nThe major landmarks in the region include the Curzon Gate (Bijoy Toran) and Sarbamangala Temple in Bardhaman, and Nava Kailash (108 Shiva Temples) and Rajbari Temple Complex in Ambika Kalna.\nEat.\nPurba Bardhaman is diverse in terms of cuisine. It is famous for confectioneries like \"sitabhog\", \"mihidana\" and \"langcha\"."}
{"id": "194556", "revid": "2253620", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=194556", "title": "Malda (district)", "text": "Malda (\u09ae\u09be\u09b2\u09a6\u09b9 \"M\u0101ldoho\" or \u09ae\u09be\u09b2\u09a6\u09be \"M\u0101ld\u0101\") is a district of West Bengal, India. It borders Uttar Dinajpur and Dakshin Dinajpur to the north, Bangladesh to the east, Murshidabad to the southwest, Jharkhand to the west, and Bihar to the northwest.\nUnderstand.\nVisitor information.\nOfficial websites:\nStay safe.\nAs it is a low-lying basin, the area is prone to flood."}
{"id": "194561", "revid": "2289947", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=194561", "title": "Viti", "text": "For the country, see Fiji\nViti (Albanian: \"Vitia\") is a city located in the Gjilan region of Kosovo.\nUnderstand.\nVitia is a city in the southeastern part of Kosovo, on the border with Macedonia, east of Ferizaj and south of Gjilan.\nThe municipality of Vitis lies in the central part of the Upper Anamorave plain, through which the Morava e Binqe River passes.\nVitia lies in the central part of the field of Upper Morava, through it passes the river Morava e Bin\u00e7a in a very fertile plain, suitable for the cultivation of all agricultural plants. After the occupation of Kosovo by the Ottoman invaders, the territory of Viti was part of the Nahi of Morava.\nGet in.\nBy bus.\nThe best way to get to Vitia is by bus through Ferizaj city (\u20ac2). You can also catch a bus from Pristina to Ferizaj City (\u20ac2), then Ferizaj to Vitia (\u20ac2)"}
{"id": "194570", "revid": "2217354", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=194570", "title": "Jiaganj", "text": "Jiaganj or Ziaganj (\u099c\u09bf\u09af\u09bc\u09be\u0997\u099e\u09cd\u099c \"Jiy\u0101gonj\"), a heritage town in Murshidabad, is popularised by former playback singer Arijit Singh, who was born here."}
{"id": "194571", "revid": "2217354", "url": "https://en.wikivoyage.org/wiki?curid=194571", "title": "Azimganj", "text": "Azimganj (\u0986\u099c\u09bf\u09ae\u0997\u099e\u09cd\u099c \"\u0100jimgonj\"), a heritage town in Murshidabad, is the district's main railway hub.\nUnderstand.\nBefore the Battle of Plasey, Murshidabad was the leading trade centre of India. It attracted a host of traders from different parts of India and beyond. Azimganj served as the settlement for the \"marwari\" traders who migrated from Rajasthan. These traders built huge mansions and temples. Today, one of these mansions has been converted into a heritage boutique hotel. Apart from the hotel guest the twin towns are also visited by day trippers from Murshidabad.\nTo the north of Azimganj lies Boronagar, known for its temples with terracotta and stucco ornamentation. \nGet around.\nAzimganj, along with the temple town of Boronagar, are mostly explored from a day trip from Murshidabad. Hotels in Murshidabad can arrange for totos for the trip. There are no bridges across the river. There is an active ferry service, which also transports vehicles. Barikothi, a heritage boutique hotel in Azimganj provides specially curated tours for their guests.\nBuy.\nSaree: Azimganj is a hub of weaving activity, known for the production of Murshidabadi silk. Visitors can buy directly from the weavers. However, the prices are almost the same as retail outlets. However, bargaining is possible.\nEat.\nThe heritage boutique hotel Bari Kothi offers a lunch package of the vegetarian \"Sheherwali\" cuisine, combining culinary traditions from Rajasthan and Bengal. The lunch also includes a property tour of the heritage hotel."}
